WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.370
Welcome to SaleLedger. This short tour uses fictional practice files, so nothing here is a real auction house.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:13.230
Start your free 30-day SaleLedger trial at the trial page.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.950
Enter your email, then the eight-digit code sent to your inbox.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.520
Copy your trial key and get SaleLedger from the Microsoft Store.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.900
No card is needed, and starting the trial won't charge you.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:37.230
Open SaleLedger. It runs in your web browser, on your own computer. The first time, read the short agreement, tick the box, and click I agree.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:45.640
Then paste your trial key and click Activate. Your ledger, bank statement and consignor data stay on this computer.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.950
Each month you need two files.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:01:06.030
One: your trust ledger, in SaleLedger's ledger template. One row per buyer deposit, consignor check, commission transfer and unpaid settlement, taken from the records you already keep. Download the template from getsaleledger.com.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:14.920
Two: your trust account bank statement, as CSV, Excel, or your bank's Quicken download. That's Option B.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:26.740
If you already have an IIF file of the trust account, Option A takes it together with an Auction Flex settlement export. This tour uses Option A with the practice files.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:34.606
Under Option A, choose the three files. Then enter the auction number, the trust account's

00:01:34.606 --> 00:01:41.074
opening balance on the first day of the period, and the as-of date. In the practice set,

00:01:41.074 --> 00:01:47.543
that's auction 246, an opening balance of one hundred fifty thousand dollars, and August

00:01:47.543 --> 00:01:50.410
thirty-first. Click Run reconciliation.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:57.403
The result is one line: In balance, or Out of balance. Below it, the three numbers that

00:01:57.403 --> 00:02:03.364
have to agree: the bank's adjusted balance, the trust control balance from your records,

00:02:03.364 --> 00:02:09.326
and the total owed to consignors. In the practice month they don't agree, and the report

00:02:09.326 --> 00:02:11.290
says so instead of hiding it.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:18.668
Every gap becomes a numbered exception in plain English. Here, a consignor check cleared

00:02:18.668 --> 00:02:24.875
for five hundred dollars more than the ledger recorded. Further down, a deposit with no

00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:30.582
matching auction, and a transfer to the operating account with no authorization.

00:02:30.582 --> 00:02:35.790
SaleLedger never guesses at an explanation. It tells you what to look at.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:45.160
The report ends with a check register: date, check number, payee, and amount, taken from the files you gave it.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.550
Keep it with the month's records.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.623
Above the report: save it as a file, export the numbers to a spreadsheet, print it or save

00:02:57.623 --> 00:03:03.494
it as a PDF, or email it to your bookkeeper. Keep a copy with the month's records, and

00:03:03.494 --> 00:03:06.770
you're audit-ready without doing anything extra.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:14.745
Next month, SaleLedger pre-fills the opening balance from the bank's actual ending balance

00:03:14.745 --> 00:03:21.315
last period, so you never retype it. Bring in three new files, click Run, and read the

00:03:21.315 --> 00:03:25.440
result. That's the whole routine. Thanks for watching.
